section.p:
        To add a JMenuBar to an SPanel, override the public JMenuBar getMenuBar() method which is originally defined in SwingView, SPanel's superclass. 

        In your implementation of getMenuBar(), create SMenuItem objects. The SMenuItem objects are 
        constructed with the Controller's String constant, which will identify the control string issued from 
        this menu item, as well as serve as the item's label. 

        Note: If the menu item's display label is to be internationalized, consideration for this 
        should be taken in the Controller's doHandle method when intercepting the control and matching its string constant.


        In the View: 

public JMenuBar getMenuBar() { 
  JMenu menu = new JMenu("Test"); 
  menu.add(new SMenuItem(MenuController.GOT_NAME_CONTROL_ID, this)); 
  menu.add(new SMenuItem(MenuController.EXIT_CONTROL_ID, this)); 

  JMenuBar menubar = new JMenuBar(); 
  menubar.add(menu); 

  return menubar; 
}

        In the Controller: 

        Provide clauses in the do handle method to handle the controls issued from the JMenuBar. 

protected void doHandleControl(Control inControl) throws ControlException { 
  if (inControl.matchesID(GOT_NAME_CONTROL_ID)) { 
    inControl.markMatched();
    doGotName(); 
  } else if (inControl.matchesID(EXIT_CONTROL_ID)) { 
    inControl.markMatched();
     doExit(); 
  } 
}
